# Audio Player

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

An audio player plays a sound clip, such as a voice message left at the front door. The browser draws the play button, the time bar and the volume control. Civica does not restyle them.

This page owns the `<audio>` element and its two children, `<source>` and `<track>`. The Video & Camera Stream Player and Picture pages use `<source>` and `<track>` too, and link here for them. For the Home Assistant media player and its status wording, see [Media playback and output selection](../home-assistant/media-playback.html).

This page covers:

- Audio Player
  - `<audio>`
    - `<source>`
    - `<track>`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

An audio player has a play and pause button, a time bar, the time played and a volume control. The controls have no visible label of their own, so the clip needs a label written beside it. Below the player, a transcript gives the same words as text.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-audio"><code>&lt;audio&gt;</code></h3>

The element that plays the clip. Its attributes are:

- `src` names the file. Leave it out when you list files with `<source>`.
- `controls` shows the player. Without it nothing is drawn, so always set it.
- `autoplay` starts the clip at once. Do not use it: browsers often block it, and sudden sound is hard on people nearby.
- `loop` repeats the clip and `muted` starts it silent.
- `preload` is `none`, `metadata` or `auto`, and says how much to fetch before play.
- `crossorigin` sets how the file is requested from another site.

Text between the tags is shown only when the browser cannot play audio. Write a short sentence there that points to the transcript.

Home Assistant uses the element in the media browser dialog, with `controls` and `autoplay`, one `<source>` and a translated sentence for browsers that cannot play it.

The Civica theme has no variable for the player, because the browser draws it. Colours, size and shape are the browser’s. A card-mod style cannot reach inside it either. A Markdown card can hold an `<audio>` element with a `<source>` element, and Home Assistant keeps the `autoplay`, `controls`, `crossorigin`, `loop`, `muted`, `preload` and `src` attributes. It removes the `<track>` element, so a Markdown card cannot add captions. The Home Assistant media player card is a different element, owned by [Media playback and output selection](../home-assistant/media-playback.html).

Give the player a name that people can hear. Point `aria-labelledby` at the visible label, as in the example.

<h3 id="child-audio--source"><code>&lt;source&gt;</code></h3>

Lists one file for an `<audio>`, `<video>` or `<picture>` element. It has no closing tag and no text of its own.

- `src` is the file.
- `type` is the file type, with its codecs if needed, such as `audio/mpeg`. The browser skips a file it cannot play and moves to the next one.
- `media`, `srcset`, `sizes`, `width` and `height` are for pictures and video. Do not use them for audio.

List the files in order of preference. A `<source>` has no meaning for a screen reader, so the label and transcript do the work.

```html
<audio controls preload="none" aria-labelledby="clip-label">
  <source src="/local/doorbell.mp3" type="audio/mpeg">
  This message cannot be played here. Read the transcript below.
</audio>
```

<h3 id="child-audio--track"><code>&lt;track&gt;</code></h3>

Adds a text track to an `<audio>` or `<video>` element, in WebVTT format. Its attributes are `kind` (`subtitles`, `captions`, `descriptions`, `chapters` or `metadata`), `src`, `srclang`, `label` and `default`.

For video, a `captions` track gives people who cannot hear the sound the words and sounds. For audio, an audio element has no picture area, so do not rely on a track to show text. Write a transcript on the page instead. Home Assistant does not use `<track>` anywhere in its own screens.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- The browser controls work with a keyboard and need no extra code.
- The controls have no visible label, so you must write one.
- You cannot change the colours, size or shape of the controls.
- Autoplay is often blocked by the browser.
- A text track does not replace a transcript for audio.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Show the controls, name the clip and say how long it lasts. Put the transcript on the same page, not behind a separate link. Do not start sound without a request, and do not play a clip for longer than three seconds without a way to pause it.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.2.1 Audio-only: a transcript gives the same information as the clip.
- 1.4.2 Audio Control: no sound starts by itself.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard and 2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap: the browser controls are the way to operate it.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: give the player a name with `aria-labelledby` or `aria-label`.


